# RUNNABLES: what this repository's own tools say they do, one verb per line.
#
# This is the SUPPLIER's half: a claim, written by whoever wrote the tool. It
# grants nothing. What a machine actually allows is its crew's answer, in
# `<profile>/GRANTS`, compiled into the settings its sessions read. The whole
# design is docs/RUNNABLES.md, after station-node's `the-runnable-manifest.md`.
#
# THE CLAIM IS A MENU, NOT A PERMISSION. A verb listed here and answered nowhere
# is never allowed, and nothing complains. A runnable file with no line here at
# all is UNDECLARED: `bin/runnables` shows it, and it matches no rule.
#
# ONE LINE PER VERB, AT ITS NARROWEST REUSABLE SHAPE. A rule cut to one exact
# invocation fires once and is a receipt; a rule for the whole tool is the hole
# the gate exists to close. The shape is what follows the holder on the command
# line, and a trailing ` *` means "and any further arguments". `-` is the tool
# with no arguments at all.
#
# Fields, separated by `|`:
#
#   holder   the file, relative to the repository root
#   run      how it is started: sh (Git Bash on Windows), python, powershell
#   shape    the verb, as above
#   class    what it touches. The class decides the DEFAULT answer, and a
#            profile may narrow it; only a merge to main widens one.
#
#              read      reads, prints, changes nothing           allow
#              inside    writes inside this checkout only         allow
#              outside   writes outside it: the host, the depot   ask
#              desk      needs a person there, or an admin grant  ask
#              secret    prints or handles a credential           deny
#              service   a job's own verb, never a session's      deny
#
#   says     one line: what it does, in the tool's own words where it has them
#
# Git Bash only to read, no yaml: this is parsed by `bin/runnables`, which has
# to run on a bay that has no Python yet.

# holder                        | run        | shape                | class   | says

# The door (kiosk-1). door.py's usage header is the source for every shape.
machines/kiosk-1/door.py        | python     | -                    | read    | is the door up
machines/kiosk-1/door.py        | python     | status               | read    | is the door up
machines/kiosk-1/door.py        | python     | screens              | read    | are the screens in node.yml up and full-screen
machines/kiosk-1/door.py        | python     | screens --launch     | outside | bring the screens up: opens windows on the panels
machines/kiosk-1/door.py        | python     | startup              | read    | what starts the door at logon, and does it point here
machines/kiosk-1/door.py        | python     | startup --xml        | read    | the logon task this checkout implies, to stdout
machines/kiosk-1/door.py        | python     | startup --install    | desk    | register that task, and retire the Startup shortcut
machines/kiosk-1/door.py        | python     | sessions             | read    | the Claude sessions that come back after a sign-in
machines/kiosk-1/door.py        | python     | sessions pin *       | outside | always or never bring a session back
machines/kiosk-1/door.py        | python     | sessions --revive    | outside | start the snapshot's sessions now
machines/kiosk-1/door.py        | python     | serve                | service | become the door, on [::]:8080. The logon task's
machines/kiosk-1/door.py        | python     | supervise            | service | run serve, restart it, pull. The logon task's
machines/kiosk-1/door.py        | python     | wifi-password *      | secret  | prints a saved network's key
machines/kiosk-1/m365-token.ps1 | powershell | *                    | secret  | prints a Microsoft 365 access token. The door calls it
machines/kiosk-1/gotcha         | sh         | *                    | inside  | append one line to GOTCHAS.log; commits on media-node only

# Which profile this checkout wears.
machines/binding                | python     | -                    | read    | which machine profile this checkout wears, if any

# Build tooling at the root. Each writes files that are committed, and nothing else.
bin/build-kiosk.py              | python     | --check              | read    | fail if kiosk/welcome.* is stale
bin/build-kiosk.py              | python     | --print              | read    | write nothing, show both
bin/build-kiosk.py              | python     | -                    | inside  | write kiosk/welcome.yml and welcome.js
bin/build-sites.py              | python     | --list               | read    | show the manifest, build nothing
bin/runnables                   | sh         | *                    | read    | this census

# The recorder trove's bay procedure (troves/recorder/README.md, machines/BAY.md).
# Bare verbs: the defaults (-Version 32.2.2, -Node editing-bay-1) are the use.
# Every step but check appends to %LOCALAPPDATA%\<profile>\bay.ndjson, so
# none of them is `read`. None needs an administrator; install and confirm are
# desk because install is where the firewall grant is placed, and confirm opens
# an OBS window on the screen of a machine people use.
troves/recorder/bay/obs-portable.ps1 | powershell | check    | read    | what is received, staged, installed; the grant; changes nothing
troves/recorder/bay/obs-portable.ps1 | powershell | receive  | outside | fetch the OBS zip and GitHub's published sha256 into the profile's bay
troves/recorder/bay/obs-portable.ps1 | powershell | verify   | outside | hash the zip against that digest; logs the result
troves/recorder/bay/obs-portable.ps1 | powershell | stage    | outside | unpack, portable-mark, gate on OBS Project signing the core
troves/recorder/bay/obs-portable.ps1 | powershell | install  | desk    | swap into the trove's prefix, cellar the previous copy, updates off
troves/recorder/bay/obs-portable.ps1 | powershell | confirm  | desk    | start it, see it kept to itself, stop it

